# In 1930, Canadian industry consumed 11,032,538 barrels of cement, with construction industry employment of ~250,000 (https://www66.statcan.gc.ca/eng/1961/196107050685_p.%20685.pdf)
# Each barrel is 171kg, for a total of 1,886,564t of cement production
# This comes out to 7.54t per worker, which we scale to 1985's 14.9t/worker, or a ratio of 50.6%.
# Scaled to 85 employment here vs. 125 in 1985, that's a ratio of 34% as much cement consumption, or ~34t of cement/month.
# In addition in 1930 the Canadian brick industry produced 319,846,711 bricks from clay, assuming 3kg/brick this is 959,540t of bricks. This comes out to 4.68 crates/worker/year which equals, for a 3000 person jobsite, 30.75 crates per simutrans month vs. 35.3 in 1985, or a ratio of 87%.
# Scaled to 85 employment/125 vs. 1985, this comes to 59% as much brick consumption as 1985, or ~21 crates/month.
# In 1985 we have 99.7+35.3=135 consumption of the 2, in 1930 we have 34+21=55 consumption, so 40% as much productivity on average.
# All this comes out to the split of cement to bricks being 42-26 instead of 50-18.
Obj=factory
name=BuildersYard1905
copyright=James
intro_year=1905
intro_month=11
retire_year=1962
retire_month=2
population_and_visitor_demand_capacity=200
employment_capacity=83
mail_demand=16
class_proportion[0]=20
class_proportion[1]=67
class_proportion[2]=13
